          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Bram Stoker</strong><br>\nBram Stoker was born on November 8, 1847, in Clontarf, Dublin, Ireland. As a child, he was bedridden with an unknown illness until he made a complete recovery at the age of seven. This experience may have influenced the gothic tones and themes of sickness and recovery in his later writing. He excelled academically and attended Trinity College, Dublin, graduating with honors in mathematics.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Stoker's Career Before Writing</strong><br>\nBefore becoming a writer, Bram Stoker had a successful career as a civil servant at Dublin Castle and as the personal assistant of actor Henry Irving. He also worked as a theatre critic for the Dublin Evening Mail. His position in the cultural scene of Dublin provided him with a valuable network of contacts in literary circles.</p>\n\n<p><strong>\"Dracula's\" Conception</strong><br>\nStoker spent several years researching European folklore and stories of vampires for his novel \"Dracula.\" The book was not an immediate bestseller, but it eventually came to be seen as a classic of gothic literature. It was published in 1897, after Stoker had moved to London and was managing the Lyceum Theatre.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
