          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Themes in \"Dracula\"</strong><br>\nDracula is known for its exploration of themes like the fear of the unknown and the clash of cultures. As Count Dracula moves from Transylvania to England, the disruption his presence causes reflects societal fears of the era. The novel also examines the constraints of Victorian morality.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Stoker's Other Literary Works</strong><br>\nBesides \"Dracula,\" Stoker wrote several other novels, including \"The Lady of the Shroud\" and \"The Lair of the White Worm.\" He also published short stories and non-fiction works. However, none of these achieved the lasting fame or influence of \"Dracula.\"</p>\n\n<p><strong>Influence on Horror Genre</strong><br>\nStoker's work has had a significant influence on the horror genre. \"Dracula\" has inspired countless adaptations in film, television, and literature. The image of the vampire he created has become the basis for vampire portrayals in modern media.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
