          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Personal Life</strong><br>\nStoker married Florence Balcombe in 1878, and they had one son, Irving Noel Stoker. His wife was previously courted by Oscar Wilde, with whom Stoker maintained a friendship despite Wilde's notorious public trial and imprisonment.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Bram Stoker's Later Years</strong><br>\nIn his later years, Stoker suffered from multiple strokes and poor health. Despite the fame of \"Dracula,\" he did not gain substantial financial success from his writing. He died on April 20, 1912, and was cremated at Golders Green Crematorium.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy of Bram Stoker</strong><br>\nBram Stoker's legacy is largely tied to \"Dracula,\" which has overshadowed his other works. He is credited with creating the modern vampire mythos, influencing an entire genre, and the novel continues to be a staple of horror fiction in schools and popular culture worldwide.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
